index c5e9ce89ddd6b268ed570a06aeba776bc6c79a61..3ccabd138129afd3a2f2a77d474a6527c7c6a4ee 100644
--- a/docs/extensions.md
+++ b/docs/extensions.md
@@ -343,6 +343,39 @@ The whole directory is covered on purpose. A static import scan cannot see
 computed `import(p)`, `require(p)`, `eval`-based import or real `createRequire`
 targets, so hashing only the entry file would claim more than it proves.
 
+### The one thing the bytes cannot cover
+
+An extension factory is ordinary code, so it may build its `steps` from data that
+is **not in its distribution** — a JSON graph in the project directory, an
+environment variable, a document it fetched. Then the workflow changes while every
+byte the distribution digest covers stays identical, and that digest alone notices
+nothing.
+
+So a task also records **what the workflow declared**: its name, its first step, its
+step names in declaration order, each step's kind and terminal status, which agent
+hooks each step defines, and whether the workflow has an `onTransit`. Names are
+encoded rather than written literally, because a step may be named anything and two
+different workflows must never serialise to the same text. A change to any of that
+stops the task the same way a changed distribution does.
+
+Be exact about what this second digest does **not** cover. It is the declared shape,
+not the behaviour: transitions, guards, caps and recovery targets are code in this
+design — the graph is decided at runtime by `onTransit`, a cap is a number the
+workflow's own code reads out of `step_visits`, and recovery is engine-wide rather
+than per workflow. Their bytes are covered by the distribution digest, and swapping
+one hook's body for another with the same signature moves that digest and not this
+one. Making them declarative would change how extensions are authored, and that is a
+design decision rather than something to settle here.
+
+A pin that carries no shape where the registry has one is refused exactly like a pin
+that carries none at all: on a task that already has a workflow, "written before this
+check existed" and "had the field removed" are indistinguishable. The same restart
+re-admits it.
+
+The shape is recorded only beside a distribution identity. A registry assembled
+without the loader stays honestly unidentified rather than becoming half-identified
+by a value derived from an object in memory.
+
 What this is not: it pins bytes on disk. It is not a sandbox, and it says nothing
 about what the code does once imported or what it fetches at runtime.
 
